## FINDING 1 — an in-progress run is counted as a success

`Recent lifecycle runs` prints its verdict from two different populations:

```js
var bad = runs.filter(r => r.status === "completed" && r.conclusion !== "success").length;
(bad ? bad + " of " + runs.length + " failed" : "all " + runs.length + " succeeded")
```

The numerator counts COMPLETED runs only; the denominator counts ALL of them.
A run in flight is therefore excluded from `bad` and included in the total, and
is asserted to have succeeded before it has finished. The failing branch carries
the same defect: "1 of 7 failed" claims six successes, one of which is unknown.

Observed four times, on four different runs, with the table one line below the
badge disagreeing with it each time:

```text
15:11  all 7 succeeded   while deploy-stage  #32  in progress
15:14  all 7 succeeded   after #32 completed successfully — THE BADGE DID NOT CHANGE
15:26  all 8 succeeded   while promote-prod  #11  in progress
15:46  all 9 succeeded   while destroy prod  #44  in progress
```

The 15:11 → 15:14 pair is the proof: the row changed from `in progress` to
`success` and the verdict above it did not move, because it had already counted
the run as a success. Had #32 failed, the badge would have flipped to `1 of 7
failed`. It does not lag — it pre-assumes.

WHY NO GATE SAW IT. `history-summary` is asserted by nothing: not
`site-page-check`, not `page-tense-check`, not `page-freshness-check`. A fixture
with an in-progress run does exist (`tests/fixtures/page-measure/in-flight/runs.json`,
`deploy-stage #64 in_progress`) — but it also contains failures, so on that
fixture the badge takes the `failed` branch and the green-while-unknown shape
never appears. Both halves matter; the second is the real reason.

## FINDING 2 — the run layer overrides the one caption that is permanent

Branch order in the node renderer, run layer FIRST:

```js
if (ownLive)        -> "running now"
else if (phaseLive) -> "its phase is running" / "which node is unknown"
else if (finished)  -> "finished in this run" / "figures publish when the cycle ends"
...
else                -> nodeTense(...)   // where `not measured here` lives
```

`nodeTense`'s own comment about these nodes is `NOTHING WILL EVER MEASURE THIS
ONE` — the image push, the migrate/seed task and the human approval are not
resources, so no timeline can carry them. While their phase runs, that permanent
truth is replaced by a temporary one.

One node, four states, one tab, fifteen minutes:

```text
15:00  phase not started   ECR push   not measured here · its step is in Actions, not in a timeline
15:07  phase running       ECR push   its phase is running · which node is unknown
15:11  phase finished      ECR push   finished in this run · figures publish when the cycle ends
15:14  cycle finished      ECR push   not measured here · its step is in Actions, not in a timeline
```

The middle two are false. The third is a PROMISE, and the cycle that made it
refuted it eleven minutes later. Same sequence observed on `migrate + seed`
(phase 3) and on `a human, in the prod environment` (phase 5, `Approve`).

The same caption served two opposite futures in the same cycle: at 15:31 all
eight terraform nodes of `Apply — prod` also read `figures publish when the
cycle ends`, and for them it was TRUE — their figures arrived at 15:38. A reader
cannot tell the two apart.

`which node is unknown` is additionally false on arithmetic:

```text
phase 5  Approve    ONE node in the phase        — "which node is unknown"
phase 8  Destroy    TWO nodes, and the page had already drawn the other one
                    as `measured · 7m 33s`       — "which node is unknown"
```

The string dates from 20c, when a running phase genuinely could not be resolved
further (Terraform creating one of eight). Per-environment binding now resolves
it; the caption never caught up.

WHY NO GATE SAW IT. `page-tense-check` lifts `nodeTense` as a pure block and
hands it data — there is even a case named
`a-node-no-timeline-can-carry-never-says-not-run-yet.json`, and it passes. The
function is correct. The defect is that while a phase runs, nothing CALLS it.
`page-freshness-check` is blind for a different reason: an open tab and a fresh
load render the same wrong caption, so they converge.

## FINDING 3 — the `destroyed` disclaimer does not cover `unknown`

```js
if (envs === "destroyed" && node.service !== "destroy") {
  return { word: "destroyed", note: "these figures are from the cycle that ended", ... };
}
return { word: "measured", note: "", ... };
```

There is no `unknown` branch, and `unknown` is the state an environment is in
whenever a run is touching it. Predicted from the code at 15:31, before it could
be observed; the observation window on the way UP was missed and is recorded as
missed. Caught on the way down, with both sides:

```text
15:44 – 15:56   prod UNKNOWN     phase 6 `measured`, eight nodes at full colour,
                                 figures printed, NO qualification —
                                 while prod was being deleted
15:56           prod DESTROYED   phase 6 `destroyed`, every node dimmed,
                                 "these figures are from the cycle that ended · 27s"
```

Same eight nodes, same figures, twelve minutes apart. The only thing that
changed is the environment's state word.

The contradiction is on ONE screen: the environment panel at 15:44 said `Last
reported values, shown for reference only` about exactly the data the map two
screens below was presenting unqualified. Inside the map itself, phase 7's
suites said `2 of 2 · from the previous run` at the same instant.

## What held, live

- **The re-read (20l, ADR-0053), against real records rather than fixtures.**
  The front moved 2 → 3 → 4 → done and the figures arrived on a tab nobody
  touched: `Apply — stage` went from `figures publish when the cycle ends` to
  `measured 8m 13s` with per-node durations, and the quality gate's
  `from the previous run` captions were replaced by this run's numbers.
- **Per-step binding (ADR-0043).** In phase 7, `seed assertion` read `running
  now · its report arrives when the step ends` while `smoke` beside it still
  showed the previous run's report; later `seed assertion` carried this run's
  `2 of 2` while `smoke` still said `from the previous run`.
- **Verdict and figures from different sources, correctly.** `API contract`
  read `passed` (this run's step) beside `52 of 52 · 14s · from the previous
  run` (the last published report).
- **Per-environment binding in a shared phase.** During `destroy prod`, only the
  prod node of phase 8 was marked live; `stage — everything above` kept
  `measured · 7m 33s` and was not dimmed or lit.
- **Tense clears both ways.** With no run in flight after #44, the suites'
  `from the previous run` captions disappeared.
- **The environment panel is careful where the map is not**: `Run #44 (destroy
  prod) is waiting and has not reported yet`, `This job has not started, so it
  reports no steps yet. That is an absence, not a failed read.`
- **The teardown order from 19f earned itself.** `Destroy ALB first (release
  ENIs before the IGW)` took 7m 8s; `Terraform destroy` after it took 0m 14s.

## The GitHub cadence is adaptive, and the page says so

A prediction made from one reading of the cadence line — "the run cannot appear
before 15:02:38, the poll is 300 s" — was wrong: it appeared at 15:00:02, 2m 11s
after dispatch. The line itself explains it:

```text
15:00   GitHub every 300 s                              budget 47 of 60
15:07   GitHub every 137 s (paced by the remaining budget)   budget 52 of 60
15:11   GitHub every  94 s                              budget 56 of 60
15:46   GitHub every  78 s                              budget 18 of 60
15:52   GitHub every 136 s   (the hour rolled over)     budget 58 of 60
15:56   GitHub every 300 s                              budget 56 of 60
```

Not a defect. Recorded because a session that reads one value of a
self-describing line as a constant will mis-predict the next one.

## What this cycle did NOT measure, stated as gaps

- **The convergence delay.** #32 finished about 15:12:30 and the next
  observation was 15:14:48, with the figures already drawn. The `max-age=60`
  ceiling is therefore bounded above by roughly two minutes and was NOT
  measured. Measuring it needs a sample every few seconds across the moment a
  run goes green.
- **The map's dating sentence.** It reads `dated 2026-08-09` and the previous
  cycle was also 2026-08-09, so a fresh date and a stale one are
  indistinguishable. Nothing can be concluded from it in this cycle.
- **`not reached yet` never appeared.** `nodeTense` emits it only when
  `!record`, and every node carried a record from the previous cycle. A cycle
  that follows a completed cycle may be structurally unable to exercise the
  mirror clause; that is a hypothesis this cycle raises and does not settle.
- **The UNKNOWN-before-apply window on the way up** (prod already `unknown`,
  phase 6 not yet running) was seen only at phase-header level. The node
  captions in that window were not captured.
